---
description: How to build SF Desk - local Mac client only when testing, every client on a deploy
alwaysApply: true
---

# Testing new features: local Mac client only

When testing a new feature, build and launch only the local Mac app. No release, no iPad install, no Windows or
iOS builds.

```bash
export PATH="/Applications/SF Desk.app/Contents/Resources/node/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin:$PATH"
osascript -e 'quit app "SF Desk"'
scripts/build-mac-app.sh --from dist/SF-Desk-mac.zip --worktree   # uncommitted changes included
rsync -a --delete "dist/SF Desk.app/" "/Applications/SF Desk.app/"
codesign --verify --strict "/Applications/SF Desk.app" && open "/Applications/SF Desk.app"
```

- `--worktree` builds the tracked files as they are, so no commit is needed. Untracked new files have to be
  `git add`ed first, or they're left out.
- The build is labeled `-dirty` and keeps the current build number. There's no version bump, PR or tag.
- If `--from` refuses because `package-lock.json` changed, rebuild the zip with `scripts/build-mac-package.sh`.

# Deploy: build all the others too

When the user asks to deploy (release), build every client:
1. Add the release note to `docs/RELEASE-NOTES.md`, then `git add -A && git commit`.
2. `osascript -e 'quit app "SF Desk"'; scripts/build-apps.sh`: bumps the build, opens and merges a PR, tags it,
   and builds the Mac app, disk image and Windows package.
3. Install the Mac app: `rsync -a --delete "dist/SF Desk.app/" "/Applications/SF Desk.app/"`, `codesign --verify
   --strict`, `open`.
4. Install to the iPad: `ios/build-device.sh 00008103-0012550636C3001E` (unlocked, on the cable; the Cisco VPN
   can block it). If it can't connect, say so and offer to retry.

Never upload to TestFlight unless the user explicitly asks.
